      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\), we first need to determine the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\).\n\nWe perform long division of 1 by 7:\n\n1. \\(1.000000 \\div 7 = 0.142857142857\\ldots\\)\n\nFrom this division, we see that the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is \\(0.\\overline{142857}\\), which means the digits \"142857\" repeat indefinitely.\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the digit within the repeating block \"142857\". We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6 (the length of the repeating block).\n\n\\[100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\\]\n\nThis tells us that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating block \"142857\". The digits in the block \"142857\" are 1, 4, 2, 8, 5, and 7. Therefore, the 4th digit is 2.\n\nThus, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).\n\nCompute the definite integral $\\int_{-1}^{1} (\\sqrt{1-x^2} + x^2) dx =$ $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\cdot$\n\nTo compute the definite integral \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} (\\sqrt{1-x^2} + x^2) \\, dx\\), we can split the integral into two separate integrals:\n\n\\[\n\\int_{-1}^{1} (\\sqrt{1-x^2} + x^2) \\, dx = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\sqrt{1-x^2} \\, dx + \\int_{-1}^{1} x^2 \\, dx\n\\]\n\nFirst, we evaluate the integral \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} x^2 \\, dx\\). Since \\(x^2\\) is an even function, the integral from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\) is twice the integral from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x^2 \\, dx = 2 \\int_{0}^{1} x^2 \\, dx = 2 \\left[ \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_{0}^{1} = 2 \\left( \\frac{1^3}{3} - \\frac{0^3}{3} \\right) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we evaluate the integral \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} \\sqrt{1-x^2} \\, dx\\). The function \\(\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\) describes the upper half of a circle with radius 1 centered at the origin. The integral \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} \\sqrt{1-x^2} \\, dx\\) is the area of the upper half of this circle, which is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore, we combine the results of the two integrals:\n\n\\[\n\\int_{-1}^{1} (\\sqrt{1-x^2} + x^2) \\, dx = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThe high point of the Monticello fan club ended last Friday. A Monticello employee announced on his radio talk show that the club had accumulated $250,000 from accumulated donations to the utility company that supplies the power to the park. It is hoped a change will take place in this matter, which would avoid another dog-eat-dog winter day. Mr. Smith made a remark concerning the matter on an earlier radio program and some of the fans questioned the influence Mr. Smith might have in helping to change the course. The manager of the club made several visits to the Smith home to discuss the matter with this member of the club. He assured him he had proved his point with a large sum of money. Mr. Smith assures that Silverthorne Park will not close for quality power, unless the Monticello Mint Company decides to use lower-end alternatives, such as power plants originally built for oil. The Monticello Mint Company is helping the track to play their role as Montana energy producer, and is sure the Monticello crowd will stick to their program.\n\nChoose your answer: based on the paragraph above can we conclude that \"The Monticello track had money problems before this story.\"?\n\nPick from: +Yes. +It's impossible to say. +No.\nI think the answer is\n\nYes",
